[SPEAKER_13]: One thing that we're severely lacking in is affordable ownership opportunities like as we all know that owning property, you know, is, you know, one of the things that you can pass, you know, generational wealth onward, and that there's been, you know, segments of society that have been really cut out of that with redlining and all that.

358
00:35:46,348 --> 00:36:15,388
[SPEAKER_13]: that if we have a small parcel that is suitable for housing, and we get calls all the time from for profit developers that want to buy our parcels, and I always say, we're not interested in selling these right now to private developers for market rate housing, that one thing that the city is fortunate with is that we actually own some land, small parcels that would be

359
00:36:15,832 --> 00:36:17,432
[SPEAKER_13]: know, kind of useless for anything else.

360
00:36:18,713 --> 00:36:21,433
[SPEAKER_13]: That could be an opportunity for homeownership.

361
00:36:22,033 --> 00:36:26,994
[SPEAKER_13]: And some of the most expensive parts of developing housing is land acquisition.

362
00:36:27,434 --> 00:36:31,055
[SPEAKER_13]: And we have a lot of land that's could be used for that.

363
00:36:32,915 --> 00:36:42,917
[SPEAKER_13]: So I see like large parcels like yes, like some of the land that was sold was unfortunate, because we'll never get it back.

364
00:36:43,537 --> 00:36:44,657
[SPEAKER_13]: But some of the small

365
00:36:45,267 --> 00:36:47,909
[SPEAKER_13]: that could be suitable for affordable housing.

366
00:36:48,670 --> 00:36:50,652
[SPEAKER_13]: I don't necessarily think that we wanna hold on to it.

367
00:36:51,292 --> 00:37:04,624
[SPEAKER_12]: Is there perhaps a number here, like if a parcel is, for example, I'm afraid to quote numbers and be wrong off the top of my head, but I believe that single family zoning is limited to 5,000 square feet.

368
00:37:05,104 --> 00:37:13,311
[SPEAKER_12]: We've been looking at parcels that are like 2,000 square, 3,000 square feet that are two next to each other that the city owns.

369
00:37:13,932 --> 00:37:25,878
[SPEAKER_12]: or 4,000 square feet, which under current zoning would not allow a single family house, but it's actually a reasonable size because a lot of the parcels, for example, in North Medford are only 3,000 square feet.

370
00:37:26,379 --> 00:37:30,461
[SPEAKER_12]: So we actually have a lot of homes on that with size parcels.

371
00:37:31,041 --> 00:37:40,746
[SPEAKER_12]: And so in that scale, selling that parcel with the deed restriction on it to be deed restricted affordable housing is a way for somebody to have home ownership.

372
00:37:41,427 --> 00:37:42,227
[SPEAKER_12]: On the other hand,

373
00:37:42,809 --> 00:37:49,071
[SPEAKER_12]: I think that we are thinking about city on land, we're thinking, more than 10,000 square feet.

374
00:37:49,411 --> 00:38:07,617
[SPEAKER_12]: We're thinking parcels over here like multi family residential parcels, and that there's some scale over which we are all agreeing that the city shouldn't be selling those quickly we should be doing very significant analysis before sale and probably what we want to be looking at is long term leases.

375
00:38:09,643 --> 00:38:24,303
[SPEAKER_12]: So I'm wondering if there's a place a number there, we could probably run some numbers with our GIS staff pretty quickly to come up with some number where we wouldn't if it needed to be if the trust wanted to sell something over.

376
00:38:26,337 --> 00:38:29,359
[SPEAKER_12]: 8,000 square feet, they would have to come back to the council for permission.

377
00:38:29,899 --> 00:38:32,301
[SPEAKER_12]: And that would engender a public process.

378
00:38:32,662 --> 00:38:36,305
[SPEAKER_12]: But if it was a small parcel, then the trust could sell it.

379
00:38:36,505 --> 00:38:47,714
[SPEAKER_12]: And if I understand correctly, and actually I'd like Shelly or Sharon to correct me on this, if we sold a parcel for affordable housing as the affordable housing trust, it would have to be deed restricted affordable housing.

380
00:38:48,094 --> 00:38:50,977
[SPEAKER_12]: So affordable in perpetuity, or is that not correct?

381
00:38:51,980 --> 00:38:52,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Shelly, are you able to speak to that?

382
00:38:53,001 --> 00:38:54,222
[SPEAKER_03]: I know you've also had your hand up.

383
00:38:54,282 --> 00:38:55,983
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, I want to say a few things.

384
00:38:56,584 --> 00:39:00,547
[SPEAKER_14]: So one, if you're using it, some of it can depend on the funding source.

385
00:39:00,727 --> 00:39:10,556
[SPEAKER_14]: The trust statute doesn't specifically say a restriction in perpetuity, but CPA funds does say that if you're acquiring a real property interest, there needs to be a restriction in perpetuity.

386
00:39:12,736 --> 00:39:16,059
[SPEAKER_14]: But we call it affordable housing because it has a restriction.

387
00:39:16,199 --> 00:39:24,585
[SPEAKER_14]: So the idea is that we also have the anti-aid amendment in Massachusetts where there needs to be a public benefit from public resources being invested.

388
00:39:24,605 --> 00:39:29,729
[SPEAKER_14]: So that's typically shown in affordable housing with an affordability restriction.

389
00:39:29,809 --> 00:39:34,612
[SPEAKER_14]: So that is basically a given that you need a restriction when you're investing in funds.

390
00:39:34,993 --> 00:39:41,478
[SPEAKER_14]: But I wanted to talk a little bit more about the land and this concern around public land.

391
00:39:42,935 --> 00:39:51,439
[SPEAKER_14]: One, in several communities, the trust is actively engaged in disposing of municipal land for affordable housing, but the land isn't always transferred to the trust.

392
00:39:52,120 --> 00:39:58,903
[SPEAKER_14]: So it doesn't necessarily need to be transferred to the trust for the trust to be actively engaged in the process working with municipal staff.

393
00:39:59,403 --> 00:40:06,547
[SPEAKER_14]: There are some communities where they are transferring parcels, Chelsea is transferring tax title parcels to the trust.

394
00:40:08,215 --> 00:40:11,436
[SPEAKER_14]: The directive is that it's to be redeveloped into affordable housing.

395
00:40:11,956 --> 00:40:26,840
[SPEAKER_14]: I would encourage you not put this restriction in the ordinance, but to consider at the time of vans, if you want to be more prescriptive about how the land is used to do it at that point but not to restrict your in the ordinance that would be my suggestion.

396
00:40:29,697 --> 00:40:35,800
[SPEAKER_14]: But the affordability restriction is that's how we identify it as affordable housing.

397
00:40:36,020 --> 00:40:38,421
[SPEAKER_14]: And again, like with CPA, it's in perpetuity.

398
00:40:39,121 --> 00:40:47,946
[SPEAKER_14]: And there could be an expectation that when the trust invests that there's a similar kind of restriction regardless of whether it's CPA funds or not.

399
00:40:49,166 --> 00:40:57,330
[SPEAKER_14]: But that is typical, that's best practice, that's creating a public benefit when you're creating affordable housing.

400
00:40:59,858 --> 00:41:09,406
[SPEAKER_10]: And if I could just, Daniel, I know that Daniel and I are working on a project where a city-owned property was previously a tax title property.

401
00:41:09,426 --> 00:41:13,069
[SPEAKER_10]: And by itself, the property is not developable.

402
00:41:13,589 --> 00:41:14,510
[SPEAKER_10]: It's a small lot.

403
00:41:15,531 --> 00:41:27,881
[SPEAKER_10]: But the owner of the abutting lot could assemble different parcels so that to make or create a bigger, larger parcel on which they could develop multiple family affordable housing units.

404
00:41:29,757 --> 00:41:35,539
[SPEAKER_10]: So there may be some parcels for which it has no value of its own.

405
00:41:37,240 --> 00:41:48,083
[SPEAKER_10]: I have seen some limitations placed by some other communities by saying if the property is worth more than, let's say, 500,000, then you have to come back to the city council.

406
00:41:48,343 --> 00:41:58,287
[SPEAKER_10]: But there are various kinds of limitations, but I would encourage you not to place a blanket limitation because, again,

407
00:41:59,002 --> 00:42:05,284
[SPEAKER_10]: there are properties that can be assembled and sold and they're always going to be deed restricted.

408
00:42:06,325 --> 00:42:13,248
[SPEAKER_10]: Nobody is going to be able to convert them to completely market rate housing and use it for profit.

409
00:42:16,149 --> 00:42:16,409
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

410
00:42:16,529 --> 00:42:17,829
[SPEAKER_06]: And I appreciate that.

411
00:42:17,849 --> 00:42:18,610
[SPEAKER_06]: That's helpful.

412
00:42:18,910 --> 00:42:24,592
[SPEAKER_06]: Um, I do generally, my main concern here is that we

413
00:42:25,948 --> 00:42:37,270
[SPEAKER_06]: quite frankly, the only way we're going to address the cost of housing crisis is removing as many units as we can permanently from the speculative market.

414
00:42:37,990 --> 00:42:39,231
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's my goal here.

415
00:42:39,831 --> 00:42:47,232
[SPEAKER_06]: So I'm open to other options and ways to do that other than just saying, you have to come back for anything.

416
00:42:47,452 --> 00:42:50,193
[SPEAKER_06]: So I'm not trying to be prescriptive.

417
00:42:50,333 --> 00:42:51,773
[SPEAKER_06]: I'm just also trying to think

418
00:42:53,348 --> 00:43:05,331
[SPEAKER_06]: 15 years when none of us are here, maybe, maybe not, you never know, that that consideration is still a part of the conversation.

419
00:43:05,471 --> 00:43:15,233
[SPEAKER_06]: So it sounded to me also like there could be temporary, and maybe I just misinterpreted, would it be possible for the trust to only require temporary deed restrictions?

420
00:43:15,253 --> 00:43:20,795
[SPEAKER_06]: Because I thought if it was CPA funded, it'd have to be in perpetuity, but perhaps other sources of funding

421
00:43:21,656 --> 00:43:28,922
[SPEAKER_06]: would only require less than, for example, a hundred years, or I know there's some properties that we have that are 50 years deed restricted.

422
00:43:29,022 --> 00:43:30,543
[SPEAKER_06]: Did I hear that right?

423
00:43:30,563 --> 00:43:30,603
[SPEAKER_06]: Or?

424
00:43:32,885 --> 00:43:41,412
[SPEAKER_14]: So it could depend on the funding source, but some funding sources may not require as long of a restriction as CPA.

425
00:43:45,856 --> 00:43:46,977
[SPEAKER_14]: So that's a possibility.

426
00:43:48,636 --> 00:43:52,297
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, I mean, at the very least, and maybe I think you kind of get where I'm going here.

427
00:43:52,437 --> 00:44:11,262
[SPEAKER_06]: So maybe, maybe you could come up with something that kind of that captures all of this in one, I certainly wouldn't want us to, you know, either convey public land to the trust or, or have it, you know, the trust be involved in conveying public land directly from the city to someone for a project that's not in perpetuity.

428
00:44:11,382 --> 00:44:12,602
[SPEAKER_06]: I think that would be a real

429
00:44:14,176 --> 00:44:25,324
[SPEAKER_06]: you know, even if there's a hundred years of public benefit on the hundred and first, I can only imagine that in a hundred years, that property is going to be worth more in Medford than it is today, barring, you know, some catastrophe.

430
00:44:26,505 --> 00:44:36,573
[SPEAKER_06]: So that would be a concern that I have as well, that we, if we are in some way conveying public land in any way, that the deed restriction would be permanent.

431
00:44:37,714 --> 00:44:38,735
[SPEAKER_06]: And I'll leave it there.

432
00:44:38,755 --> 00:44:41,236
[SPEAKER_06]: I may make a motion on this a little later.

433
00:44:43,974 --> 00:44:51,556
[SPEAKER_05]: Um, can we require any project funded through the trust be subject to the prevailing wage laws?

434
00:44:54,376 --> 00:44:55,917
[SPEAKER_03]: Attorney Everett, are you able to speak to that?

435
00:44:57,417 --> 00:45:04,959
[SPEAKER_10]: You know, the trust is a public body and it's a board of the city, so whatever laws apply to the city apply to the trust.

436
00:45:06,979 --> 00:45:08,600
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, so say we solicit

437
00:45:10,510 --> 00:45:17,627
[SPEAKER_05]: project for affordable units that we're going to fund, but we're not going to own.

438
00:45:17,647 --> 00:45:20,394
[SPEAKER_05]: We're using public dollars to fund a private project, right?

439
00:45:21,685 --> 00:45:23,486
[SPEAKER_05]: That private project could be done by anybody.

440
00:45:24,486 --> 00:45:26,567
[SPEAKER_05]: We have a responsible employer ordinance.

441
00:45:26,587 --> 00:45:28,428
[SPEAKER_05]: We have a wage theft ordinance here in the city.

442
00:45:29,948 --> 00:45:33,750
[SPEAKER_05]: I know ultimately, you know, I'm a strong believer in prevailing wage.

443
00:45:34,270 --> 00:45:49,216
[SPEAKER_05]: And I think that if we're going to be using public funds to build affordable housing units, the people that are building units should be paid affordable living wage so that they could actually be people that don't have to live in affordable housing units and can purchase homes in this community, live in this community.

444
00:45:49,696 --> 00:46:01,384
[SPEAKER_05]: So I think that that's important to look at if there's a way that we could ensure that any project that's funded through these public dollars is subject to the public prevailing wage requirements.

445
00:46:01,464 --> 00:46:04,326
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that would be something that would be beneficial and rise everybody up.

446
00:46:07,027 --> 00:46:15,793
[SPEAKER_05]: The workers will be getting their rate pay and the dollars will be going towards creating affordable housing but also creating wealth for individuals.

447
00:46:16,714 --> 00:46:18,775
[SPEAKER_03]: Shelly has her hand up if you're able to speak to this, Shelly.

448
00:46:19,347 --> 00:46:20,267
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, I just want to jump in.

449
00:46:20,307 --> 00:46:25,049
[SPEAKER_14]: I'm not the most articulate person in responding to this, and there might be someone else on the call.

450
00:46:25,109 --> 00:46:28,830
[SPEAKER_14]: But if you do that, it makes affordable housing significant.

451
00:46:29,070 --> 00:46:34,871
[SPEAKER_14]: It can make affordable housing significantly more expensive, which means more public subsidy, more local public subsidy would be needed.

452
00:46:35,472 --> 00:46:38,853
[SPEAKER_14]: It could put projects at a disadvantage to get additional resources.

453
00:46:38,913 --> 00:46:41,073
[SPEAKER_14]: You want to think about that really thoughtfully.

454
00:46:41,093 --> 00:46:47,395
[SPEAKER_14]: And I think touch base with people who are more expert in this area.

455
00:46:48,034 --> 00:46:51,697
[SPEAKER_14]: because affordable housing is already extremely expensive per unit.

456
00:46:51,737 --> 00:46:57,661
[SPEAKER_14]: It already takes a lot of public subsidy and the more requirements we put on it, then the more expensive it gets.

457
00:46:58,982 --> 00:47:15,974
[SPEAKER_10]: Right, and I would add to what Shelly said by saying that such a requirement could jeopardize any financing projects because it's going to make the project much more expensive or there can be banks and other lenders who won't finance a project with such a requirement.

458
00:47:16,900 --> 00:47:20,502
[SPEAKER_10]: So, I mean, a trust is supposed to promote affordable housing.

459
00:47:20,742 --> 00:47:24,904
[SPEAKER_10]: This may be holding them back in developing projects.

460
00:47:28,325 --> 00:47:30,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we're going to be chasing our tail.

461
00:47:30,146 --> 00:47:37,049
[SPEAKER_05]: I have a problem with public dollars and paying somebody less than prevailing wage or allowing somebody to use public dollars to pay somebody less than prevailing wage.

462
00:47:37,489 --> 00:47:37,849
[SPEAKER_03]: I agree.

463
00:47:37,889 --> 00:47:39,450
[SPEAKER_03]: And I absolutely agree with your point.

464
00:47:40,482 --> 00:47:46,385
[SPEAKER_03]: people that are funding, you know, doing the project are them going to need affordable housing that we need more affordable housing because you know, we're stuck in a vicious cycle.

465
00:47:46,405 --> 00:47:47,185
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

466
00:47:47,225 --> 00:47:49,066
[SPEAKER_06]: And we've been building substandard properties.

467
00:47:49,286 --> 00:47:51,607
[SPEAKER_06]: We see what happens when properties are built with substandard.

468
00:47:52,448 --> 00:47:58,290
[SPEAKER_05]: And also we do have Briscoe here who's kind enough to take some time off of his day to come over here and join us.

469
00:47:59,071 --> 00:48:08,135
[SPEAKER_05]: I just like to hear from him a little bit as to how he thinks that he could envision a affordable housing trust fund in Medford, help the housing authority and their initiatives and what they're trying to do.

470
00:48:08,972 --> 00:48:15,035
[SPEAKER_05]: and what role you could see the housing authority playing in this once it comes to fruition.

471
00:48:15,776 --> 00:48:16,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Councilman.

472
00:48:16,416 --> 00:48:21,039
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, for those on the call, Executive Director of Medford Housing Authority, Jeffrey Driscoll, is here.

473
00:48:21,119 --> 00:48:32,064
[SPEAKER_03]: So if you want to speak to how perhaps you've seen other communities work on this as well, I'm curious after Jeffrey speaks, Shelley and Sharon, if you have any thoughts on how a housing authority may be involved in such a trust.

474
00:48:32,845 --> 00:48:34,946
[SPEAKER_07]: Thank you, Madam President for inviting me.

475
00:48:35,906 --> 00:48:36,647
[SPEAKER_07]: I appreciate that.

476
00:48:37,435 --> 00:48:39,417
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Knight, thank you for suggesting that I come.

477
00:48:40,077 --> 00:48:41,038
[SPEAKER_07]: I've not seen the ordinance.

478
00:48:42,119 --> 00:48:48,905
[SPEAKER_07]: I will say, I'll give you a little bit of a background on where the housing authority is at this point and how we may be able to fit in with the housing trust.

479
00:48:49,546 --> 00:48:55,151
[SPEAKER_07]: I'll preface that by saying that I don't want the housing trust to be in competition with the housing authority.

480
00:48:55,991 --> 00:49:03,418
[SPEAKER_07]: And I do hear that it may be relative to funds that we have received previously from the CPC.

481
00:49:04,260 --> 00:49:08,383
[SPEAKER_07]: But the Housing Authority right now is renovating 121 Riverside 11 story building.

482
00:49:09,324 --> 00:49:12,887
[SPEAKER_07]: We have a budget that was presented me today.

483
00:49:12,907 --> 00:49:14,628
[SPEAKER_07]: I'm $143 million.

484
00:49:14,668 --> 00:49:17,531
[SPEAKER_07]: We're going to gut the entire building.

485
00:49:18,131 --> 00:49:24,116
[SPEAKER_07]: We're going to relocate the residents who were there to other units that the Housing Authority has.

486
00:49:24,496 --> 00:49:26,478
[SPEAKER_07]: We also have a master plan there.

487
00:49:27,022 --> 00:49:39,870
[SPEAKER_07]: That master plan in includes increasing the number of units there from 200 to 217, which will do within the confines of the, the building, also on that site.

488
00:49:40,807 --> 00:49:50,657
[SPEAKER_07]: We have a master plan which shows the development of an additional 80 plus units in a four story building with a podium underneath for parking on that site.

489
00:49:51,178 --> 00:49:54,121
[SPEAKER_07]: We've already met with the city officials relative to that.

490
00:49:55,042 --> 00:50:00,408
[SPEAKER_07]: I have been before or provided information to the council previously on walkway and court.

491
00:50:01,022 --> 00:50:06,524
[SPEAKER_07]: We have presently a $15 million application to the state for funding.

492
00:50:06,964 --> 00:50:12,105
[SPEAKER_07]: We're one of four housing authorities as finalists for that type of funding.

493
00:50:12,625 --> 00:50:18,047
[SPEAKER_07]: We anticipate that 144-unit development will increase to 238 units, 198 elderly, and 40 family, all deeply affordable.

494
00:50:27,095 --> 00:50:43,043
[SPEAKER_07]: The housing authority in addition to that is looking at the pre village on Riverside Avenue, we have a physical needs assessment that we're undertaking to see where we can increase the number of units there and increase the quality of the housing.

495
00:50:43,694 --> 00:50:48,617
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't look at a housing trust as necessarily being adverse to the interests of the authority.

496
00:50:49,277 --> 00:50:56,822
[SPEAKER_07]: But the authority, I would think, would be able to assist the housing trust because of our professional staff.

497
00:50:57,782 --> 00:51:01,084
[SPEAKER_07]: I know there was some concerns that were raised about seven volunteers.

498
00:51:01,845 --> 00:51:06,407
[SPEAKER_07]: My board is made up of five volunteers, and I don't speak for them today because they're not familiar with this.

499
00:51:06,788 --> 00:51:08,869
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't speak from my own point of view.

500
00:51:09,490 --> 00:51:17,556
[SPEAKER_07]: I have a professional staff that is dealing with the consultants, the construction consultants, the architects, in dealing with these projects.

501
00:51:17,596 --> 00:51:25,201
[SPEAKER_07]: The total amount of the two projects that we have funding for already for mass development, first is the Riverside project.

502
00:51:25,261 --> 00:51:27,042
[SPEAKER_07]: As I said, that's $143 million.

503
00:51:27,402 --> 00:51:30,364
[SPEAKER_07]: And we estimated that walk and court would be approximately $140.

504
00:51:30,444 --> 00:51:35,748
[SPEAKER_07]: But seeing the prices come in, I think it's going to be much more significant than that.

505
00:51:36,068 --> 00:51:38,310
[SPEAKER_07]: And we're doing that in-house with the use of consultants.

506
00:51:39,350 --> 00:51:48,855
[SPEAKER_07]: My hope would be that if a housing trust were to be authorized by this body, that there would be some participation by the authority.

507
00:51:48,895 --> 00:51:55,079
[SPEAKER_07]: I've had just a very limited discussion with the city officials, both the mayor and Danielle.

508
00:51:55,879 --> 00:52:04,584
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't know that the housing authority should be a member of that committee, of the trust itself, but certainly the housing authority would be very well positioned

509
00:52:05,084 --> 00:52:08,746
[SPEAKER_07]: to be of assistance with the development of affordable housing.

510
00:52:10,226 --> 00:52:34,436
[SPEAKER_07]: I'll end on this one note, and that is that in all the years I've been involved in public housing, what I've seen is that the most difficult aspect for things to be affordable is not in getting the initial financing, not even in building the units or repairing the units, but is having that ongoing funding to be able to afford those units.

511
00:52:34,984 --> 00:52:40,049
[SPEAKER_07]: and the Housing Authority with its repositioning of assets as additional subsidy.

512
00:52:41,009 --> 00:52:52,980
[SPEAKER_07]: For example, at 121 Riverside, by the efforts that we're undertaking, we'll have 200 additional subsidies to be able to provide for affordable housing in the city of Medford.

513
00:52:53,344 --> 00:53:05,009
[SPEAKER_07]: And those 200 vouchers in part are going to assist us to be able to renovate Walkland Court so that the Housing Authority is very much a resource for the development of affordable housing.

514
00:53:05,329 --> 00:53:09,210
[SPEAKER_07]: Would we be interested in a 3,000 square foot parcel of land?

515
00:53:09,730 --> 00:53:16,533
[SPEAKER_07]: More than likely not, but we can make a significant impact on affordable housing in the city of Medford.

516
00:53:16,813 --> 00:53:18,614
[SPEAKER_07]: And quite frankly, we're at a point right now

517
00:53:19,274 --> 00:53:21,597
[SPEAKER_07]: through the planning stages that we're doing that.

518
00:53:22,017 --> 00:53:26,842
[SPEAKER_07]: So do I think that a housing trust is something that is a negative?

519
00:53:26,942 --> 00:53:27,683
[SPEAKER_07]: Absolutely not.

520
00:53:28,604 --> 00:53:36,212
[SPEAKER_07]: But I think it needs to be done in concert with the housing professionals that we are at the Medford Housing Authority to be able to be of assistance.

521
00:53:37,754 --> 00:53:39,295
[SPEAKER_07]: And just as a note,

522
00:53:41,231 --> 00:53:58,390
[SPEAKER_07]: relative to what consulate night just said, and that is that the Housing Authority is going to propose procurement relief and a home rule petition to the legislature at some point in time, and that will come through the city hall, but that procurement relief is going to be a great assistance to us.

523
00:53:58,850 --> 00:54:01,433
[SPEAKER_07]: We're not looking to impact the wage rates.

524
00:54:02,532 --> 00:54:03,412
[SPEAKER_07]: the prevailing wage.

525
00:54:04,232 --> 00:54:06,853
[SPEAKER_07]: So that's how we're proposing to go forward with it.

526
00:54:06,993 --> 00:54:17,016
[SPEAKER_07]: And it'll make a significant difference in the ability for us to go forward with not only the Saltonstall project at 121, but also Walkman Court and eventually LaPree Village.

527
00:54:18,856 --> 00:54:19,176
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

528
00:54:19,896 --> 00:54:20,416
[SPEAKER_03]: Senator Fevella?

529
00:54:20,977 --> 00:54:22,377
[SPEAKER_08]: You brought up a great point, Mr. Driscoll.

530
00:54:22,417 --> 00:54:23,417
[SPEAKER_08]: First, thank you for being here.

531
00:54:23,837 --> 00:54:24,477
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you.

532
00:54:25,018 --> 00:54:30,479
[SPEAKER_08]: But it looks like and sounds like the trust

533
00:54:31,408 --> 00:54:33,270
[SPEAKER_08]: is really focused on CPC money.

534
00:54:35,952 --> 00:54:41,818
[SPEAKER_08]: So, do we know if we can, can we get a number of what

535
00:54:45,073 --> 00:54:59,664
[SPEAKER_08]: what the monies over the last few years that have gone to the Housing Authority and how that would affect future funding from the CPC to the Housing Authority because I you brought up a good point.

536
00:55:00,324 --> 00:55:03,607
[SPEAKER_08]: And I think that's something that I wasn't even thinking of.

537
00:55:04,347 --> 00:55:16,290
[SPEAKER_07]: I can tell you off the top of my head if I may, Madam President, back a few years ago, we presented a proposal to the CPC for $452,000 for a conversion from oil to gas.

538
00:55:17,030 --> 00:55:22,052
[SPEAKER_07]: And that was based upon security issue, and we had the fire department support in doing that.

539
00:55:22,812 --> 00:55:25,833
[SPEAKER_07]: We, with the city had put forth

540
00:55:26,593 --> 00:55:36,635
[SPEAKER_07]: an application, I believe it was for $60,000 for bocce courts that were originally going to be built on city land, and it was built at 121 Riverside, which is the housing authority land.

541
00:55:37,175 --> 00:55:45,777
[SPEAKER_07]: And just recently, the CPC had provided us $350,000 as a pre-development monies for Walklane Court.

542
00:55:45,977 --> 00:55:52,219
[SPEAKER_07]: That's estimated to cost us 1.1 million, and the CPC was kind enough to give us $350,000 toward that.

543
00:55:55,010 --> 00:56:07,456
[SPEAKER_08]: So thank you, and my question is probably for Danielle, how will that affect as we move forward, if we go through with this ordinance now, how does this, I mean, there's only one funding mechanism.

544
00:56:07,516 --> 00:56:20,882
[SPEAKER_13]: I mean, we would love people to donate property and monies, but this is what... Nothing stops the trust from giving funds to the housing authority.

545
00:56:21,282 --> 00:56:24,244
[SPEAKER_13]: They would just be, the trust would be the ones

546
00:56:25,509 --> 00:56:31,312
[SPEAKER_13]: that everyone would go to for housing projects versus going to the CPC.

547
00:56:35,334 --> 00:56:43,758
[SPEAKER_05]: I think it's also important to point out, I believe it's what, 10% annually is required to go into each bucket, right?

548
00:56:43,778 --> 00:56:47,040
[SPEAKER_05]: So the idea of the Affordable Housing Trust was that we have to dedicate

549
00:56:47,749 --> 00:56:49,430
[SPEAKER_05]: 10% every year, put it somewhere.

550
00:56:49,450 --> 00:56:53,794
[SPEAKER_05]: So why don't we put that money into the Affordable Housing Trust and they can administer it, do something with it.

551
00:56:54,214 --> 00:57:07,685
[SPEAKER_05]: We still have a lot more money that can be used for housing initiatives that sit in the CPA account that the CPC can still oversee and keep exclusive from the Affordable Housing Trust, right?

552
00:57:08,325 --> 00:57:09,106
[SPEAKER_13]: Yeah, we could do that.

553
00:57:09,406 --> 00:57:10,787
[SPEAKER_13]: Yeah, we could do it a number of ways.

554
00:57:10,827 --> 00:57:14,150
[SPEAKER_05]: Like I know, I keep bringing up- It's not like the Affordable Housing Trust is just getting all the CPA money.

555
00:57:14,976 --> 00:57:17,597
[SPEAKER_13]: No, some cities do do it that way.

556
00:57:17,617 --> 00:57:19,937
[SPEAKER_13]: They say 40%, yes.

557
00:57:22,298 --> 00:57:31,200
[SPEAKER_13]: So there's just a number of ways that we can do it, but I don't see how this would keep us from funding housing authority projects at all.

558
00:57:36,922 --> 00:57:41,803
[SPEAKER_10]: There are some CPC funds cannot be used for certain projects such as

559
00:57:42,523 --> 00:57:49,570
[SPEAKER_10]: They can't be used to repair existing housing unless the housing was created or developed using CPA funds.

560
00:57:50,972 --> 00:58:03,985
[SPEAKER_10]: So there may be certain limitations in helping the housing authority, whereas the Affordable Housing Trust can use CPA funds for a wider variety of purposes.

561
00:58:05,679 --> 00:58:26,965
[SPEAKER_10]: So we can assist the housing authority with acquisition and development of affordable housing, but we cannot help them with the rehabilitation or improvement of existing housing because that's prohibited by the CPA.

562
00:58:27,085 --> 00:58:32,567
[SPEAKER_10]: However, the trust could use those funds for other purposes that are permitted under the CPA.

563
00:58:34,132 --> 00:58:35,432
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Attorney Everett.

564
00:58:35,732 --> 00:58:36,713
[SPEAKER_03]: Shelly, you also had your hand up.

565
00:58:37,473 --> 00:58:45,134
[SPEAKER_14]: Yep, I just wanted to speak a little bit to the housing authority and having another housing entity, creating a trust, having another housing entity in the community.

566
00:58:45,194 --> 00:58:51,976
[SPEAKER_14]: And at MHP, we strongly urge communities to have their housing entities work collaboratively.

567
00:58:51,996 --> 00:59:00,958
[SPEAKER_14]: We would expect the trust when you create one, when it's up and running, for them to work upfront with the housing authority

568
00:59:01,610 --> 00:59:12,475
[SPEAKER_14]: We would urge your municipal staff to help facilitate those conversations as well with the CPC to make sure that these different boards are working together that they're not.

569
00:59:13,997 --> 00:59:30,327
[SPEAKER_14]: trying to do the same thing, that they're not stepping on each other's toes, but instead that each entity is identifying its particular niche and its role in the community, and that you're all moving forward, identified housing goals for Medford, but not in a silo.

570
00:59:31,068 --> 00:59:32,829
[SPEAKER_14]: The trust is a municipal entity.

571
00:59:32,889 --> 00:59:34,750
[SPEAKER_14]: It's not on its own, out on its own.

572
00:59:35,391 --> 00:59:37,552
[SPEAKER_14]: The mayor has to be involved in it.

573
00:59:37,612 --> 00:59:40,594
[SPEAKER_14]: The city council, hopefully to some degree, will be involved in it.

574
00:59:42,482 --> 00:59:50,831
[SPEAKER_14]: the housing authorities should not see this as a competitive entity, but just another housing entity to be addressing the severe housing needs that you have in Medford.

575
00:59:53,014 --> 00:59:53,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Shelley.

576
00:59:53,554 --> 00:59:54,155
[SPEAKER_03]: Councilor Collins.

577
00:59:56,297 --> 00:59:57,178
[SPEAKER_11]: Thank you, President Marillyn.

578
00:59:57,639 --> 01:00:00,522
[SPEAKER_11]: Thank you so much, Mr. Driscoll, for being here, for sharing your perspective.

579
01:00:02,224 --> 01:00:02,905
[SPEAKER_11]: I just wanted to briefly

580
01:00:03,828 --> 01:00:08,189
[SPEAKER_11]: comment on some of the speakers, some of the recent speakers.

581
01:00:09,169 --> 01:00:21,292
[SPEAKER_11]: I just wanted to emphasize and thank you for making the point that it sounds like this is a situation where, you know, the proposed affordable housing trust and the MHA could have a symbiotic relationship.

582
01:00:21,352 --> 01:00:22,472
[SPEAKER_11]: And I thank Ms.

583
01:00:22,512 --> 01:00:33,395
[SPEAKER_11]: Evans for speaking to this, that there's enough complexity in how the affordable housing trust can maneuver the different buckets that the CPC funding has to go into that it's not,

584
01:00:33,975 --> 01:00:39,163
[SPEAKER_11]: it doesn't have to be a zero-sum game and either we fund the AMHA, which I think we're all

585
01:00:39,782 --> 01:00:43,584
[SPEAKER_11]: extremely grateful for your tenure and your expertise in our community.

586
01:00:44,345 --> 01:00:50,568
[SPEAKER_11]: And we can, you know, explore the many funding streams for an AHT at the same time.

587
01:00:51,389 --> 01:00:53,530
[SPEAKER_11]: So that's really, it's great to get some clarity around that.

588
01:00:54,090 --> 01:01:02,555
[SPEAKER_11]: And I missed making this point several minutes ago before Mr. Driscoll spoke, but I just wanted to note, I also am interested in, you know, I don't know if,

589
01:01:04,696 --> 01:01:13,282
[SPEAKER_11]: Maybe the attorneys on the call or staff here today could speak more to this but in speaking about our housing situation in Medford with some of our.

590
01:01:13,886 --> 01:01:15,387
[SPEAKER_11]: know, local leaders in the labor community.

591
01:01:15,427 --> 01:01:25,694
[SPEAKER_11]: I know that the issue of wage justice, prevailing wage in affordable housing construction is something that's deeply felt by our leaders in the labor community.

592
01:01:25,754 --> 01:01:35,901
[SPEAKER_11]: And I totally understand the sort of oroboros effect of more expensive to develop affordable housing, you know, and when we have such a strong need to develop that pipeline.

593
01:01:35,921 --> 01:01:42,585
[SPEAKER_11]: But at the same time, I think that that's an avenue that I really think we should consider before taking it off of the table.

594
01:01:43,066 --> 01:01:43,266
[SPEAKER_11]: Thank you.

595
01:01:43,638 --> 01:01:44,398
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Councilor Collins.

596
01:01:44,458 --> 01:01:45,179
[SPEAKER_03]: Councilor Carvalho.

597
01:01:46,059 --> 01:01:46,799
[SPEAKER_09]: Thank you, Madam President.

