01:01:49,160 --> 01:01:52,021
[SPEAKER_09]: Not for you, Jeff, but Alicia or Danielle.

599
01:01:52,821 --> 01:01:55,362
[SPEAKER_09]: I don't know if this has been asked before, but I think it's been asked in the past.

600
01:01:56,423 --> 01:02:00,004
[SPEAKER_09]: Can you provide the Council a list of all these properties that are available in the city?

601
01:02:01,705 --> 01:02:05,426
[SPEAKER_09]: Are they the small lots that you have?

602
01:02:06,934 --> 01:02:16,176
[SPEAKER_09]: I think we may have asked that at some time ago, I don't know if it ever got provided, but going forward, do we have a list of the properties that are available for sale that the city owns?

603
01:02:17,456 --> 01:02:21,637
[SPEAKER_13]: Yes, we certainly can provide a list.

604
01:02:22,237 --> 01:02:29,359
[SPEAKER_13]: One of the things that we wanted to do was to, and was a recommendation in the housing production plan, is to go out and evaluate them.

605
01:02:30,679 --> 01:02:34,180
[SPEAKER_13]: Because some of them, it's a cliff, you can't do anything with it.

606
01:02:36,152 --> 01:02:48,716
[SPEAKER_13]: I've been slowly going and actually visiting most of the parcels, walking around, bushwhacking through the woods to kind of see what would be possibly developable.

607
01:02:48,876 --> 01:03:01,860
[SPEAKER_13]: And most of the people that are inquiring about the parcels, I mean, thankfully that kind of tips us off of which ones are actually good to be developed because private developers want them.

608
01:03:02,524 --> 01:03:07,025
[SPEAKER_13]: So they kind of do some of the work for me by identifying the good ones.

609
01:03:09,186 --> 01:03:12,087
[SPEAKER_13]: But yeah, so it's a process.

610
01:03:12,167 --> 01:03:17,349
[SPEAKER_09]: If you could provide us a list of the ones that you have at the moment, even at the moments and update them as you get them.

611
01:03:17,669 --> 01:03:19,029
[SPEAKER_09]: So we know where they are.

612
01:03:19,589 --> 01:03:27,212
[SPEAKER_12]: So one thing just to be clear is that it is easy for us to get a report from the assessor's office of all the parcels we own.

613
01:03:27,612 --> 01:03:31,633
[SPEAKER_12]: The difficulty is that they are all identified by these lot ID numbers.

614
01:03:32,153 --> 01:03:33,454
[SPEAKER_12]: and not street addresses.

615
01:03:33,954 --> 01:03:44,038
[SPEAKER_12]: So if we were to just give you a spreadsheet, it wouldn't be very meaningful because the identifiers, we've been looking at them and trying to kind of give what are the roads they're on.

616
01:03:44,138 --> 01:03:46,099
[SPEAKER_12]: Some of them are in the middle of areas.

617
01:03:46,119 --> 01:03:54,242
[SPEAKER_12]: So one of the things we have to sort of figure through is, we can generate that list, we could probably mail it to you tomorrow, I think.

618
01:03:55,273 --> 01:04:12,521
[SPEAKER_12]: It's the mailing it to you with information that's actually meaningful it asking our GIS person to actually generate a map, showing which ones are owned by the city might actually be more helpful, because then you can see where they are.

619
01:04:12,541 --> 01:04:15,543
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah, I see where they are and you know what neighbors there in and with.

620
01:04:15,923 --> 01:04:16,183
[SPEAKER_09]: Right.

621
01:04:16,203 --> 01:04:18,024
[SPEAKER_12]: There's one called Zero Fulton Street.

622
01:04:18,105 --> 01:04:19,806
[SPEAKER_12]: It is not at the end of Fulton Street.

623
01:04:19,906 --> 01:04:23,028
[SPEAKER_12]: It is a random lot in the middle of Fulton Street, for example.

624
01:04:23,168 --> 01:04:30,753
[SPEAKER_12]: So that's why I just want to, yeah, wanting to get it to you in a format that is, makes sense, if that's what you want.

625
01:04:30,773 --> 01:04:31,333
[SPEAKER_09]: No, no, I understand.

626
01:04:31,353 --> 01:04:31,734
[SPEAKER_09]: I understand.

627
01:04:32,054 --> 01:04:44,502
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah, like I say, if you have identified some, if you can give us what the ones you've already identified, but as you identify them, if you can keep the council updated, so we know, you know, where these parcels are and what kind of pushback we're going to get when

628
01:04:45,081 --> 01:04:53,307
[SPEAKER_09]: We pilot, you know, we try to develop them because, you know, you may have neighbors that don't want development on an undersized lot beside them.

629
01:04:53,928 --> 01:04:54,088
[SPEAKER_12]: Right.

630
01:04:54,328 --> 01:04:59,272
[SPEAKER_12]: And we've also been looking at, some have water on them, some have wetlands, some have streams above.

631
01:04:59,632 --> 01:05:02,054
[SPEAKER_12]: Like there's all kinds of, some have cliffs.

632
01:05:02,434 --> 01:05:08,679
[SPEAKER_12]: And we may all be aware that 10 years ago, developing property with cliffs on it was not cost effective in Medford.

633
01:05:09,079 --> 01:05:12,682
[SPEAKER_12]: And now it turns out that blasting in the city of Medford is cost effective.

634
01:05:13,222 --> 01:05:14,263
[SPEAKER_12]: So some of these things,

635
01:05:14,827 --> 01:05:16,308
[SPEAKER_12]: weren't developable.

636
01:05:17,009 --> 01:05:19,571
[SPEAKER_12]: And now they are becoming so I understand.

637
01:05:19,951 --> 01:05:20,732
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah, thank you.

638
01:05:20,772 --> 01:05:20,912
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah.

639
01:05:21,192 --> 01:05:23,574
[SPEAKER_09]: If you can please send us what you have available at the moment.

640
01:05:23,974 --> 01:05:27,137
[SPEAKER_09]: And as you go forward, you know, maybe, you know, maybe maybe months or so.

641
01:05:28,157 --> 01:05:29,278
[SPEAKER_09]: Just just keep a surprise.

642
01:05:29,318 --> 01:05:36,664
[SPEAKER_09]: So, so, so we know what kind of pushback we're going to get from the from the neighborhood or particular neighbors.

643
01:05:37,284 --> 01:05:38,025
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

644
01:05:38,625 --> 01:05:40,427
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Councilor Carvalho.

645
01:05:40,467 --> 01:05:43,792
[SPEAKER_03]: Councilor Collins, Vice-Chair Bares, we are starting to bump against the 7 p.m.

646
01:05:43,812 --> 01:05:44,313
[SPEAKER_03]: meeting time.

647
01:05:44,333 --> 01:05:50,401
[SPEAKER_03]: I just want to note that we'll keep going, but as we do have a number of revisions, we will like to meet on this again.

648
01:05:51,021 --> 01:05:53,485
[SPEAKER_03]: So there will be more time for discussion, but going to Councilor Collins.

649
01:05:53,998 --> 01:05:54,879
[SPEAKER_11]: Thank you, President Morrell.

650
01:05:55,039 --> 01:05:59,662
[SPEAKER_11]: And to pick up on a thread that Vice President Behrs made earlier, so sorry if I'm stepping on your toes.

651
01:06:00,382 --> 01:06:10,829
[SPEAKER_11]: I was hoping that if it were amenable to you, maybe to make a motion for your staff going back to the issue of the conveyance of public lands, ways to solidify that.

652
01:06:12,289 --> 01:06:20,114
[SPEAKER_11]: If your office could come up with a recommendation for perhaps a square footage threshold or another metric under which you would recommend that

653
01:06:21,495 --> 01:06:36,482
[SPEAKER_11]: Um, these conveyances or sales should go forward and over which should could be subject to City Council approval to make sure that we have kind of a another check built in regarding the sale of larger publicly owned blocks.

654
01:06:37,062 --> 01:06:37,282
[SPEAKER_12]: Right.

655
01:06:37,382 --> 01:06:41,684
[SPEAKER_12]: We'll work with Attorney Everett with Sharon on that as well.

656
01:06:42,184 --> 01:06:42,464
[SPEAKER_11]: Thank you.

657
01:06:43,345 --> 01:06:44,025
[SPEAKER_06]: Is that a motion?

658
01:06:45,178 --> 01:06:45,358
[SPEAKER_11]: Yes.

659
01:06:45,858 --> 01:06:51,341
[SPEAKER_06]: And that was actually what I had my hand up for.

660
01:06:51,941 --> 01:06:53,122
[SPEAKER_06]: So I have two motions here.

661
01:06:53,142 --> 01:07:00,845
[SPEAKER_06]: I actually did, just in case we don't have Mr. Driscoll next time, I do have one question just for you, for you, Jeff, and then I have a similar additional motion.

662
01:07:02,987 --> 01:07:06,973
[SPEAKER_06]: Just if the MHA weren't to serve on the on the board itself.

663
01:07:08,314 --> 01:07:19,450
[SPEAKER_06]: What what do you kind of envision as a relationship between the housing authority and the housing trust and is it more just like open communication and dialogue and having that or I'm just interested to hear what your thoughts are.

664
01:07:20,702 --> 01:07:37,429
[SPEAKER_07]: Without having seen the audience, it's difficult for me to answer that with specificity, but I would say generally, the housing authority could actually provide the technical expertise that the council was concerned about earlier by having seven individuals without the expertise to go forward.

665
01:07:38,149 --> 01:07:45,112
[SPEAKER_07]: We certainly would be interested in working with the trust to maybe assist in the development of affordable housing.

666
01:07:45,672 --> 01:07:49,714
[SPEAKER_07]: um the housing authority right now um has its own non-profit.

667
01:07:50,054 --> 01:07:53,315
[SPEAKER_07]: We also have um two LLCs for the Riverside project.

668
01:07:53,335 --> 01:07:56,576
[SPEAKER_07]: We have three LLCs for the for the Walker and Court project.

669
01:07:56,656 --> 01:08:02,699
[SPEAKER_07]: So, um we're diversified and and certainly could be of assistance to the trust if the council so wished it to be.

670
01:08:03,579 --> 01:08:06,740
[SPEAKER_06]: Okay, thank you very much and yeah, I think we'll sounds like we're coming back to it.

671
01:08:06,960 --> 01:08:10,082
[SPEAKER_03]: Um yeah and I did, I forwarded you a copy of the ordinance just so you have.

672
01:08:10,142 --> 01:08:10,902
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you very much.

673
01:08:10,922 --> 01:08:11,182
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course.

674
01:08:11,222 --> 01:08:11,582
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry for

675
01:08:14,529 --> 01:08:19,633
[SPEAKER_06]: So in addition to councilor Collins motion, which I think, I think we're on the same page.

676
01:08:19,693 --> 01:08:30,439
[SPEAKER_06]: I'm basically just that sailor conveyance of any publicly owned parcel that has previously been conveyed to the trust, some sort of square footage amount over which the council would have a review.

677
01:08:30,459 --> 01:08:43,007
[SPEAKER_06]: I also have another motion to request language that the housing trust require any project involving public land, either previously conveyed to the trust or owned by the city.

678
01:08:43,447 --> 01:08:51,312
[SPEAKER_06]: hold a permanent affordable deed restriction, ideally with a preference for a long-term land lease in lieu of sale or conveyance.

679
01:08:51,913 --> 01:09:00,579
[SPEAKER_06]: So I'll back off on that requirement, but I do think that the permanent deed restriction really needs to be the minimum that we do.

680
01:09:00,759 --> 01:09:02,040
[SPEAKER_06]: So that would be my other motion.

681
01:09:02,380 --> 01:09:02,720
[SPEAKER_11]: Seconded.

682
01:09:02,740 --> 01:09:04,481
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you have a number of motions on the floor?

683
01:09:04,521 --> 01:09:06,443
[SPEAKER_03]: I do have one resident with their hand up on Zoom.

684
01:09:06,463 --> 01:09:08,064
[SPEAKER_05]: I'd just like to make one motion as well.

685
01:09:08,084 --> 01:09:08,964
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, Councilor Knight.

686
01:09:08,984 --> 01:09:11,646
[SPEAKER_05]: That we amend the paper to reflect the prevailing wage requirements.

687
01:09:12,247 --> 01:09:12,547
[SPEAKER_05]: Second.

